WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:03.150
GenAI’s progress is jagged.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.580
Experts can help steer a chatbot
towards the most useful part

3
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.100
of its training data.

4
00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.750
But however good the guidance is,
a model’s performance still varies

5
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.600
a lot from task to task.

6
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.950
We’re often told that GenAI
is improving so fast.

7
00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.650
But most advances have come where
answers can be checked automatically,

8
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.550
like maths, coding and visual processing.

9
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.050
So training can run in a closed loop
with no human needed to check each answer.

10
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.150
Many real-world tasks aren’t like that.

11
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:43.450
In May 2026, Cambridge researchers
found that Claude, GPT and Gemini models

12
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:49.550
matched human degree classifications
for essays only 35 to 63% of the time,

13
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.100
overmarking weak essays
and undermarking strong ones.

14
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.600
So it’s important to judge
chatbots task by task.

15
00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.050
Otherwise, we may overestimate them
where they’re still weak,

16
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:03.400
and underestimate them where they’re
genuinely getting stronger,

17
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.710
like 3D modelling and animation,
which can be very useful

18
00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.250
for creating teaching resources.

19
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.450
Try this.

20
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:13.800
Test a chatbot on a task you’ve already
completed in your area of expertise.

21
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.600
Give it your best attempt with your
expertise and documents guiding it.

22
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.450
Then compare its output to your own.

23
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.850
Even with the best steering,
where does it still fall short?
